WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:06.099
The science and practice of enhancing human
performance for sport, play, and life.

00:00:07.119 --> 00:00:07.500
Welcome

00:00:08.099 --> 00:00:08.659
to Perform.

00:00:10.119 --> 00:00:12.899
I'm Dr. Andy Galpin. I'm a
professor and scientist

00:00:13.039 --> 00:00:15.059
and the executive director of the
Human Performance Center

00:00:15.599 --> 00:00:21.959
at Parker University. Today's guest is Dr. Herman Pontzer.
Herman has a PhD in biological anthropology

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:24.619
and is currently a professor at
Duke University

00:00:24.939 --> 00:00:28.180
in the area of evolutionary anthropology. In
today's show, you're gonna learn a

00:00:28.359 --> 00:00:33.520
lot about what Herman is probably most famous for, and
that is how metabolism really works.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:37.939
We talk at length, and you'll learn a tremendous
amount about how to properly think of

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:43.459
energy expenditure, the role that exercise does or
maybe surprisingly doesn't have

00:00:43.899 --> 00:00:45.559
in things like energy expenditure,

00:00:45.959 --> 00:00:51.539
body weight, body composition, and overall health. He
also does a wonderful job teaching

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:55.439
about how energy expenditure changes
with things like pregnancy

00:00:55.799 --> 00:00:59.840
and other things we don't typically think of as
exercise or sport, but are highly

00:01:00.199 --> 00:01:01.739
relevant and incredibly interesting

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:06.919
regarding how our metabolism and overall physiology works.
So there's a lot of practical tips here.

00:01:07.099 --> 00:01:08.959
There's a lot of just
fascinating information.

00:01:09.439 --> 00:01:12.899
I know you'll enjoy it. I was thrilled to
do it. I waited a long time

00:01:13.359 --> 00:01:17.819
to talk to Herman. So with that said, please enjoy
today's episode with Dr. Herman Pontzer.

00:01:18.239 --> 00:01:20.600
Dr. Herman Pontzer, thank you for
coming in today.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:21.539
It's fun to be here.

00:01:21.539 --> 00:01:26.399
It is a huge honor. I've been wanting to talk to you for a
really long time, even before I had a, a podcast.

00:01:26.399 --> 00:01:27.079
(laughs)

00:01:27.079 --> 00:01:32.259
Your, your work is incredibly interesting. Um, I'd
actually like to start with exactly your work-

00:01:32.259 --> 00:01:33.259
Yeah.

00:01:32.259 --> 00:01:33.040
... in the sense that,

00:01:33.639 --> 00:01:37.399
uh, your PhD is in, uh,
anthropology from Harvard.

00:01:37.399 --> 00:01:38.119
That's right. Yep.

00:01:38.119 --> 00:01:43.459
Um, but you probably are most, I'd say, notable for setting
the world on fire a little bit with, um,

00:01:43.899 --> 00:01:46.239
some interesting and controversial
takes on metabolism.

00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:46.719
Mm-hmm.

00:01:46.719 --> 00:01:50.599
How the body burns fat, the role of f-
physical exercise. You had a,

00:01:51.319 --> 00:01:56.860
a series of papers come out that, um, I'd say put you on
the map, if that's a fair way to put it. So,

00:01:57.739 --> 00:02:03.280
what is it exactly that you do? Why is a anthropologist, uh,
changing the way we think about metabolism?

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:06.360
That's a great question. Uh, so, you know,

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:10.539
as an anthropologist, I'm interested in how the human
body works and how it got this way, right?

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:10.599
Yeah.

00:02:10.599 --> 00:02:14.459
So sort of an evolutionary lens on the way
the body functions. Uh, and

00:02:14.939 --> 00:02:18.500
so coming up through my training in undergrad and
then graduate school, uh, I never

00:02:18.639 --> 00:02:23.500
had any sort of public health or sort of, you know,
obesity research sort of interests necessarily.

00:02:24.039 --> 00:02:26.240
I wanted to understand energy
expenditure though, because

00:02:26.459 --> 00:02:30.019
it's, it's just sort of this... It's where the
rubber hits the road, you know?

00:02:30.019 --> 00:02:30.440
Yeah.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:34.839
Uh, any organism is, uh... You can think of
it as a strategy for turning

00:02:35.059 --> 00:02:38.019
energy into offspring, right? That's
the game in life.

00:02:38.559 --> 00:02:43.659
And so, uh, from undergraduate up through graduate
school, I was very interested in how the body

00:02:44.119 --> 00:02:47.459
brings energy in and burns it off. Uh, and

00:02:48.199 --> 00:02:52.979
you start following your nose that way, and you get into
some interesting places, because, you know,

00:02:53.099 --> 00:02:54.499
trying to understand how locomotion burns

00:02:54.559 --> 00:02:57.420
calories and how that relates to anatomy,
that was my graduate work.

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:57.999
Okay.

00:02:57.999 --> 00:03:01.559
Uh, and then when I kind of thought I understood
that pretty well, I, I built like a...

00:03:01.779 --> 00:03:05.999
Put together a mathematical model for predicting the
energy expenditure, you know, to, to go

00:03:06.539 --> 00:03:10.359
walk or run a mile depending on how
your limb anatomy looks.

00:03:10.359 --> 00:03:11.579
Right.

00:03:11.579 --> 00:03:13.299
Uh, I felt like I understood that
pretty well, and I

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:16.179
thought, "Okay, let's, let's, let's expand that. Let's
think about a whole energy budget."

00:03:16.499 --> 00:03:20.759
How does a whole, you know, a, a chimp or a human or any...
How do we bring energy in and burn it off

00:03:20.919 --> 00:03:22.999
over 24 hours, the whole, the
whole shooting match?

00:03:23.579 --> 00:03:24.019
And, um,

00:03:24.939 --> 00:03:26.139
s- uh, turns out

00:03:26.759 --> 00:03:31.139
people hadn't really been doing a lot of measurements, uh,
you know, of total energy expenditure, all

00:03:31.759 --> 00:03:33.819
energy burned through the whole day, um,

00:03:34.339 --> 00:03:38.559
in humans outside of like Western context. We had data
from the US or Europe. We didn't have-

00:03:38.559 --> 00:03:38.879
Yeah.

00:03:38.879 --> 00:03:40.779
... data from traditional contexts which
are sort of more relevant

00:03:40.839 --> 00:03:43.059
for, you know, human
evolutionary perspectives.

00:03:43.059 --> 00:03:43.399
Yeah.

00:03:43.399 --> 00:03:47.719
We had no data on, uh, the other apes or that kind of
thing. And so I started this whole... The

00:03:47.819 --> 00:03:51.739
last 20 years has been trying to, to measure
energy expenditures in these contexts

00:03:52.139 --> 00:03:56.819
from an e- ecological, evolutionary perspective.
And then, you know, we wi- wind

00:03:56.859 --> 00:03:58.459
up in some pretty interesting territory.

00:03:58.459 --> 00:03:59.519
Your first book-

00:03:59.519 --> 00:03:59.739
Mm-hmm.

00:03:59.739 --> 00:04:02.099
... Burn, walks like through a lot of
these things, but just-

00:04:02.099 --> 00:04:02.459
Oh, sure.

00:04:02.459 --> 00:04:08.479
... as a starting place in the story here, um, what, what, what
is this deal with this hunter-gatherer group?

00:04:08.479 --> 00:04:08.499
Yeah.

00:04:08.499 --> 00:04:11.499
What did we get wrong with the paleo
diet? Like give us the-

00:04:11.499 --> 00:04:11.879
Oh. Yeah.

00:04:11.879 --> 00:04:13.519
Give us the real quick overview there.

00:04:13.519 --> 00:04:17.319
So, you know, again, from an evolutionary perspective,
how do we understand our body? Well,

00:04:17.419 --> 00:04:21.179
we are hunter-gatherers, right? Humans have been hunting
and gathering since before we were humans.

00:04:21.179 --> 00:04:21.359
Yeah.

00:04:21.359 --> 00:04:24.359
Right? 2 million years. Um, and
our species is only

00:04:24.439 --> 00:04:26.539
300,000 years old. So that's, you
know... We are a

00:04:26.579 --> 00:04:29.099
hunting ga- and gathering species. That's
the ecological context.

00:04:29.579 --> 00:04:32.059
And so if you wanna understand how
the body works, um,

00:04:32.359 --> 00:04:36.399
you know, California's fine, and, uh, New
York's fine, but more relevant-

00:04:36.399 --> 00:04:36.559
Sure.

00:04:36.559 --> 00:04:38.159
... would be a hunting and gathering context.

00:04:38.699 --> 00:04:39.279
Uh, and so

00:04:39.839 --> 00:04:43.479
luckily, there are still a handful of communities that are
still doing that, you know. And they're not,

00:04:43.919 --> 00:04:47.499
they're not like time machines of the past or anything like
that, but they give us a sense of, "Okay, if

00:04:47.559 --> 00:04:51.939
you had this lifestyle in this ecological context,
this is how the body would function."

00:04:52.399 --> 00:04:53.819
They allow you to ask that question.

00:04:54.079 --> 00:04:55.999
And so nobody had measured
energy expenditures

00:04:56.259 --> 00:05:00.959
in a hunter-gatherer group before. Um, I had some
colleagues that, that were working

00:05:01.259 --> 00:05:03.899
in northern Tanzania with a, a
community called the Hadza.

00:05:04.739 --> 00:05:07.479
And so, uh, we went there and did
this work. And so

00:05:08.279 --> 00:05:08.559
w- we

00:05:09.539 --> 00:05:11.499
used this technique called doubly
labeled water, which

00:05:11.579 --> 00:05:14.039
is this isotope tracking technique
that's a really, you know,

00:05:14.639 --> 00:05:17.139
accurate measurement of how many...
Uh, it's actually,

00:05:17.239 --> 00:05:20.299
um, measures how much carbon dioxide the body makes every
day. So that tells you how many calories.

00:05:20.759 --> 00:05:24.779
It's over about a week or, or two-week period, so it's a really
good look at how many calories you're burning.

00:05:25.379 --> 00:05:29.319
And we wrote those initial grants, uh, to go do this
project, because we wanted to document

00:05:30.039 --> 00:05:32.399
how... Like, just the huge amount of
calories that we were sure

00:05:32.579 --> 00:05:36.319
r- we were sure they were burning. 'Cause they get more
activity in a day than most Americans get in a week.

00:05:36.319 --> 00:05:36.699
Right.

00:05:36.699 --> 00:05:36.979
Right?

00:05:36.979 --> 00:05:37.839
Right.

00:05:37.839 --> 00:05:41.739
And so... And we were like, "Well, that's the way that
humans used t- everywhere used to live.

00:05:42.619 --> 00:05:46.399
Let's understand what that does to the body." It's... The
calorie expenditures are gonna be huge.

00:05:46.699 --> 00:05:49.419
Nobody's mea- measured it. It was exciting
to go do this work, right?

00:05:50.179 --> 00:05:54.419
And so we go there to northern
Tanzania, 2009 and 2010, uh,

00:05:54.519 --> 00:05:57.599
armed with, you know, isotopically
enriched water and-

00:05:57.599 --> 00:05:58.599
Mm-hmm.

00:05:57.599 --> 00:06:00.859
... the whole kit. Uh, and we do
these measurements. Um,

00:06:01.459 --> 00:06:05.219
and we get back and we get the data
set back, um, and

00:06:06.099 --> 00:06:07.799
we're just completely shocked, because,

00:06:08.479 --> 00:06:13.319
you know, actually, it turns out the total energy
burned per day was no different

00:06:13.839 --> 00:06:17.879
than folks in the US, Europe, uh, you know, s-
people sitting at their desks all day.

00:06:17.879 --> 00:06:18.119
Uh-huh.

00:06:18.119 --> 00:06:20.479
Like, what's going on? And, uh...

00:06:20.479 --> 00:06:20.499
Uh-huh.

00:06:20.499 --> 00:06:22.659
And so, you know, you have a choice
there as a scientist.

00:06:23.619 --> 00:06:25.379
You have this model of how the world works.

00:06:26.039 --> 00:06:28.439
It is totally crushed by the data.

00:06:28.439 --> 00:06:29.299
(laughs)

00:06:29.299 --> 00:06:31.199
You know? And do you stick with
the, the model

00:06:31.719 --> 00:06:33.239
or do you f- say, "Okay, these are the

00:06:33.279 --> 00:06:35.899
data. We're gonna follow the data and say
this, this is the observations."

00:06:36.559 --> 00:06:38.979
Obviously, you check and make sure you didn't make any
silly mistakes, all that kind of stuff.

00:06:38.979 --> 00:06:40.719
Yeah, I think there's a little bit of a
reckoning in your soul, right?

00:06:40.719 --> 00:06:40.739
Yeah.

00:06:40.739 --> 00:06:42.479
You're like, "Oh my God, our whole
project is wr-"

00:06:42.479 --> 00:06:43.219
(laughs)

00:06:43.219 --> 00:06:44.559
We've, we've messed something up.

00:06:44.559 --> 00:06:45.359
That's exactly it.

00:06:45.359 --> 00:06:45.599
Right?

00:06:45.599 --> 00:06:45.619
Yeah.

00:06:45.619 --> 00:06:48.539
And then you j- double and you triple and
you standardize again-

00:06:48.539 --> 00:06:48.659
Yes. Yes.

00:06:48.659 --> 00:06:50.299
... and the data don't change, and now...

00:06:50.299 --> 00:06:50.799
Yes.

00:06:51.339 --> 00:06:55.339
Yes. You know, and so what do you do? And so I
thought, "Well, okay, here we go."

00:06:55.779 --> 00:06:57.959
I guess I'm, you know, gonna talk
about obesity now.

00:06:57.959 --> 00:06:57.999
Yeah.

00:06:57.999 --> 00:07:00.679
So I said... 'Cause that was this idea in
public health, of course. You still

00:07:00.739 --> 00:07:03.299
hear it. Oh, we're so sedentary
now, which is true,

00:07:03.779 --> 00:07:05.899
and that's bad. That's true. And, um,

00:07:06.219 --> 00:07:09.179
and now we're not burning up as many calories, and
that's why obesity is a problem.

00:07:09.779 --> 00:07:12.439
Well, that part turns out maybe
not to be true. And

00:07:12.519 --> 00:07:15.119
so let's think about... You know, so I
wrote the paper and, and, um,

00:07:15.819 --> 00:07:18.899
you know, and then all of a sudden, like you say, I'm in
this space talking to people like you.

00:07:18.899 --> 00:07:21.439
When you say energy expenditure-

00:07:21.439 --> 00:07:21.679
Yes.

00:07:21.679 --> 00:07:23.439
... and you're saying things like

00:07:24.039 --> 00:07:25.859
these people who are hunter-gatherers

00:07:26.119 --> 00:07:28.719
in, in various parts of Africa... And
you've done that work in, in

00:07:28.819 --> 00:07:30.839
Tanzania, in Kenya, and you've sort
of been all over.

00:07:30.839 --> 00:07:31.519
Yeah.

00:07:31.519 --> 00:07:34.039
And you say that it's not different than
us sitting around all day.

00:07:34.039 --> 00:07:34.259
Yeah.

00:07:34.259 --> 00:07:37.079
What is energy expenditure? What are you
actually talking about here?

00:07:37.079 --> 00:07:42.519
Yes. Good place to start. So, you've got about 37
trillion cells in your body, give or take.

00:07:42.519 --> 00:07:43.059
Just a few.

00:07:43.059 --> 00:07:46.059
Yeah. Nobody's counting them, but you
know, it's about that. And so

00:07:46.159 --> 00:07:48.859
every one of those cells is like a little
factory, right? It's got a

00:07:49.199 --> 00:07:52.359
set of jobs it does. It brings resources
in. It does its thing. And

00:07:52.439 --> 00:07:54.779
so every little factory is
burning energy, right?

00:07:55.199 --> 00:08:00.299
And so all of that cumulatively is what we
call your metabolic rate, right?

00:08:00.739 --> 00:08:03.139
Um, and we can think about all the
energy that you burn

00:08:03.499 --> 00:08:08.220
over the course of...... a minute while you're on the treadmill.
We can think about it w- while you're, uh, at

00:08:08.339 --> 00:08:13.319
rest, you know, for an hour. We can think about it over the
course of 24 hours. And so, the way that we usually

00:08:13.799 --> 00:08:16.819
measure it is over 24 hours. Actually, we
measure it, we measure it over a week

00:08:16.919 --> 00:08:21.359
or more, and then we take the average, sort
of per 24-hour period. Um,

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:24.539
and so that's your metabolic rate. And so, it's
all your systems altogether.

00:08:25.059 --> 00:08:27.539
One of the things that people kind of
misunderstand initially

00:08:28.259 --> 00:08:29.939
is that they think, "Oh, well, your energy

00:08:29.979 --> 00:08:33.059
expenditure is all about how active you
are." Even if you're active,

00:08:33.699 --> 00:08:35.660
most of your calories are being
burnt on other things.

00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:35.979
Yep.

00:08:35.979 --> 00:08:39.959
Your immune system, your brain, you know,
all the... you know. So, uh,

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:44.619
anyway, so it's, it's the full package.
It's the whole... all

00:08:44.679 --> 00:08:48.080
the things that your bodies are doing. That's your, your
metabolic rate that we're talking about.

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:48.459
Mm-hmm.

00:08:48.459 --> 00:08:52.479
You know, not the per minute cost on a treadmill, not while
you're just sleeping, but the whole thing.

00:08:52.479 --> 00:08:53.819
Right. So if you were to say

00:08:54.979 --> 00:08:57.019
all the calories you burn in a week, uh-

00:08:57.019 --> 00:08:57.119
Yeah.

00:08:57.119 --> 00:09:00.159
... some of them will be burned
just staying alive.

00:09:00.159 --> 00:09:00.619
Yep.

00:09:00.619 --> 00:09:04.140
Right. We, we call that generally your basal metabolic
rate or your resting metabolic rate, right?

00:09:04.199 --> 00:09:04.219
Mm-hmm.

00:09:04.219 --> 00:09:05.519
That's one of the components.

00:09:05.519 --> 00:09:05.880
Yeah.

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:07.860
Then the calories that you burn working out-

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:08.359
Yep.

00:09:08.359 --> 00:09:11.120
... which is generally, what, 5, 10%?

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:17.359
It depends on how much you work out. So, uh, it can be 30%
of your expenditure if you're active. Yeah.

00:09:17.359 --> 00:09:22.259
Okay. So somewhere, um... basal metabolic rate
is generally, what, 40, 50, 60%?

00:09:22.259 --> 00:09:24.199
Usually over half, a little over half. Yep.

00:09:24.199 --> 00:09:24.419
Yep.

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:27.839
Then on top of that, you've got some
additional calories you burn from

00:09:28.820 --> 00:09:30.499
twitching and fidgeting and like-

00:09:30.499 --> 00:09:30.820
Mm-hmm.

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:33.259
That's, what, another 5 or 10%?

00:09:33.259 --> 00:09:35.779
Well, it's hard to put numbers on that. So that's
where it starts to break down 'cause-

00:09:35.779 --> 00:09:35.839
Right.

00:09:35.839 --> 00:09:36.980
... it's really hard to figure
that out, right?

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:38.399
This, this is where you've made
your money, right?

00:09:38.399 --> 00:09:40.759
Yeah. I guess that's right. Yeah. And
so, I mean, right now

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:46.000
talking to you, I'm burning more calories than I was
in the, you know, the Uber over here

00:09:46.579 --> 00:09:48.140
chilled out zoning out. Right?

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:48.619
Right.

00:09:48.619 --> 00:09:52.419
Um, and so we wouldn't call this physical activity,
but my heart rate is higher, right? My

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:54.599
sympathetic tone is higher, right? All
those things. And so

00:09:54.679 --> 00:09:57.219
I'm burning more calories just by being
alert and attentive to you.

00:09:57.879 --> 00:09:59.779
A little bit of good stress happening, right?

00:09:59.779 --> 00:10:00.379
Hopefully.

00:10:00.379 --> 00:10:04.319
I think so. And so, th- things like that burn
calories too, and it is actually

00:10:04.559 --> 00:10:09.039
quite hard. One of the next things we wanna try to do is to
understand how that works 'cause that's... I think

00:10:09.739 --> 00:10:11.119
that's where the body can really juggle

00:10:11.819 --> 00:10:16.279
and, and, and can make space for somebody who's
really physically active to still somehow

00:10:16.559 --> 00:10:19.599
have more or less the same energy expenditure per day
as somebody who's, who's sedentary

00:10:20.159 --> 00:10:22.919
'cause they're juggling these sort of, you
know, these fungible things.

00:10:22.919 --> 00:10:27.439
Okay. Tremendous. So, when you say something like
these hunter-gatherer groups and it-

00:10:27.439 --> 00:10:27.539
Yeah.

00:10:27.539 --> 00:10:28.779
... Hadza is the one you worked out-

00:10:28.779 --> 00:10:29.779
Right.

00:10:28.779 --> 00:10:32.379
... it actually doesn't really matter, right? W- We're
actually just thinking conceptually about-

00:10:32.379 --> 00:10:32.659
Yes.

00:10:32.659 --> 00:10:34.279
... how the body regulates energy.

00:10:34.279 --> 00:10:34.519
Yeah.

00:10:34.519 --> 00:10:35.939
You have that one example, but

00:10:36.939 --> 00:10:37.179
whatever.

00:10:37.179 --> 00:10:40.359
We replicated other places and yeah,
it's a general concept.

00:10:40.359 --> 00:10:40.739
Right.

00:10:40.739 --> 00:10:41.359
Yeah.

00:10:41.359 --> 00:10:41.559
So,

00:10:42.139 --> 00:10:44.079
when you say they have the same
