Yeah. Yeah. So, um,

01:09:33.683 --> 01:09:35.703
so it could just be, you know,

01:09:36.563 --> 01:09:39.003
that you lose muscle mass and your
metabolic rate goes down for that.

01:09:39.003 --> 01:09:39.283
Yeah.

01:09:39.283 --> 01:09:42.723
But we correct for that, and we still see it.
Oops. Okay. Next explanation.

01:09:43.283 --> 01:09:43.563
Um,

01:09:44.203 --> 01:09:47.603
if you do basal metabolic rate measurements
along with this, which we

01:09:47.703 --> 01:09:51.223
have in that same dataset, you see that,
you know, for a given

01:09:52.383 --> 01:09:56.963
fat-free mass, so for a given organ sizes, you... Whole
organ size and muscle mass, the same.

01:09:57.543 --> 01:10:00.203
Metabolic, basal metabolic rates
even at rest are lower

01:10:00.723 --> 01:10:05.803
pa- post 60. And so the only way you can get
that mechanistically, uh,

01:10:06.303 --> 01:10:09.163
is for the cells themselves to
be slowing down.

01:10:10.063 --> 01:10:12.063
Um, now you're asking a different
question, I think.

01:10:12.063 --> 01:10:12.823
Mm-hmm.

01:10:12.823 --> 01:10:15.343
Related but different, which is if
I'm physically active,

01:10:16.103 --> 01:10:19.003
and one thing that'll happen is I'll keep
my muscle mass up maybe.

01:10:19.363 --> 01:10:25.323
But the other thing that might happen is, um, again,
exercise is a regulatory behavior.

01:10:25.323 --> 01:10:25.883
Yup.

01:10:25.883 --> 01:10:28.503
Right? And I'm signaling to my body to keep

01:10:28.843 --> 01:10:31.623
going and, you know, it... I don't... That's
a very hand-wavy way to talk

01:10:31.703 --> 01:10:34.683
about it. But there's something about
that regulation of activity

01:10:35.583 --> 01:10:38.163
that might keep all your cells more active.

01:10:38.163 --> 01:10:38.343
Yeah.

01:10:38.343 --> 01:10:39.263
I... Uh,

01:10:39.903 --> 01:10:43.443
okay. I'd love it. Let's test that. I mean, I
think that's possible. Uh,

01:10:43.583 --> 01:10:45.163
I don't think we would know that
for sure right now.

01:10:45.163 --> 01:10:48.663
Generally, we associate aging
with inefficiency

01:10:49.483 --> 01:10:50.623
at the cellular level.

01:10:50.623 --> 01:10:50.983
Yup.

01:10:50.983 --> 01:10:52.303
Right? So, you're losing

01:10:53.163 --> 01:10:56.263
either actual cell mass. Uh,
things are dying.

01:10:56.263 --> 01:10:56.323
Mm-hmm.

01:10:56.323 --> 01:10:59.723
Breaking off, going down, or they're becoming
hyper-inefficient, more leakage.

01:10:59.723 --> 01:11:01.143
Yeah.

01:11:01.143 --> 01:11:04.863
Which I would think would actually then make
energy expenditure higher.

01:11:04.863 --> 01:11:06.103
Mm-hmm.

01:11:06.103 --> 01:11:07.163
What am I missing here?

01:11:07.163 --> 01:11:08.023
I think

01:11:08.603 --> 01:11:12.883
you could have two things happening. You can have
inefficiencies and just lower output overall.

01:11:13.823 --> 01:11:14.563
Right? I mean,

01:11:15.203 --> 01:11:16.923
again, the only way you can make
the math work

01:11:17.443 --> 01:11:21.623
on these measurements of expenditure, and you get it from both
modalities, so it's not just a methods thing-

01:11:21.623 --> 01:11:21.903
Yeah.

01:11:21.903 --> 01:11:25.423
... is that it has to be the case that

01:11:26.523 --> 01:11:29.423
a liver ce-... You know, uh, let's just say, to pick
an organ, I don't know if this is

01:11:29.783 --> 01:11:32.503
the right one. A liver cell in a
70-year-old burns

01:11:32.583 --> 01:11:35.543
fewer calories every day than the liver
cell in a 40-year-old.

01:11:35.543 --> 01:11:35.603
Yeah.

01:11:35.603 --> 01:11:35.703
And

01:11:36.303 --> 01:11:38.343
there's... It kind of has to be that.

01:11:38.343 --> 01:11:38.923
Yeah.

01:11:38.923 --> 01:11:39.223
Um,

01:11:39.903 --> 01:11:42.843
now is it, is it the liver, or is it a different
organ? And how... What's the...

01:11:42.983 --> 01:11:44.363
That's the interesting set of questions.

01:11:44.363 --> 01:11:45.863
Yeah. But in... One of them-

01:11:45.863 --> 01:11:46.043
Yeah.

01:11:46.043 --> 01:11:47.663
... is... It's gonna have to be one
or a combination.

01:11:47.663 --> 01:11:47.743
Yeah.

01:11:47.743 --> 01:11:49.183
Something has to be changing.

01:11:50.723 --> 01:11:52.303
You think this is a function of

01:11:54.403 --> 01:11:59.003
looking at, I would not say exclusively, but
primarily cross-sectional data-

01:11:59.003 --> 01:11:59.023
Hmm.

01:11:59.023 --> 01:12:02.763
... uh, versus having, you know, more longitudinal
thi- things like that. Is that-

01:12:02.763 --> 01:12:07.423
No. That's a f- that's a fair point. I mean, these are big
cross-sectional studies. Um, I don't think

01:12:08.223 --> 01:12:14.423
you could... I, I think there has to be a longitudinal signal in
there to see the big data trends that we see.

01:12:15.123 --> 01:12:20.223
Um, but it is true for all sorts of obvious reasons that
we don't have data from somebody who's 40

01:12:20.423 --> 01:12:24.103
and then 50 and then 60 and then 70. We, um... That
would be a study that's still happening

01:12:24.763 --> 01:12:26.663
and started 40 years ago, right? We
don't have that yet.

01:12:26.663 --> 01:12:28.363
Yeah. But s-... I mean, jeez, we
should, right?

01:12:28.363 --> 01:12:28.603
I agree.

01:12:28.603 --> 01:12:29.263
We started, right?

01:12:29.263 --> 01:12:30.663
I agree. I agree. And, and we're, um...

01:12:31.043 --> 01:12:32.103
That would be something we'd really, be

01:12:32.223 --> 01:12:37.383
really interested in, is sort of, you know, gets you at 55,
gets you at 60, gets you at 65, and see.

01:12:37.383 --> 01:12:38.963
What is the longest that we do
have? Do you know?

01:12:39.063 --> 01:12:41.723
Do we have any two-year studies? Do we
have any five-year studies-

01:12:41.723 --> 01:12:42.723
Yeah.

01:12:41.723 --> 01:12:42.543
... that have-

01:12:42.543 --> 01:12:45.923
We do. We have two and five-year studies here
and there. Small ones, uh,

01:12:46.143 --> 01:12:51.123
but not-... that I know of in that critical
post-60 age group. The ones I know of

01:12:51.643 --> 01:12:54.283
are in people's sort of, you know, prime

01:12:54.743 --> 01:12:58.123
adult years. And that's interesting. There,
you find individual differences

01:12:58.583 --> 01:13:01.663
are pretty static. So, if I
measure you today and I

01:13:01.743 --> 01:13:05.244
see that you have a high metabolic rate, and I
measure you again in two years, I'll see

01:13:05.343 --> 01:13:06.744
that you have a high metabolic rate again.

01:13:06.744 --> 01:13:07.263
Mm-hmm. Mm-hmm.

01:13:07.263 --> 01:13:11.923
And it kinda... It even, even independent of what you
report anyway is lifestyle change.

01:13:12.523 --> 01:13:14.783
If you're slow, you're slow. If you're fast,
you're fast. Now, the good news

01:13:14.883 --> 01:13:17.543
is, it doesn't seem to have a big impact

01:13:17.683 --> 01:13:20.543
on whether you've gained weight or lost
weight in the intervening years.

01:13:20.543 --> 01:13:21.183
Yeah.

01:13:21.183 --> 01:13:24.863
So, that's another kind of myth, right? People think,
"Oh, my metabolic rate's high. That's why

01:13:24.943 --> 01:13:29.584
I keep the weight off." Probably what you are
experiencing is a better relationship with food-

01:13:29.584 --> 01:13:29.863
Uh-huh.

01:13:29.863 --> 01:13:31.803
... that you aren't being pushed to
overeat the same way

01:13:31.863 --> 01:13:34.963
that somebody who says, "Oh, I have a
slow metabolic rate," is

01:13:35.823 --> 01:13:39.343
maybe more food-focused and is... has a different
relationship with food. And is, is having

01:13:39.424 --> 01:13:43.043
a hard time that way. It might not be meta-... In
fact, it's not metabolic rate. I'll say

01:13:43.203 --> 01:13:46.163
that. It's... Right? And the way...
Here's, here's my metaphor for

01:13:46.223 --> 01:13:50.564
that. You walk into a bar, you wanna
play some darts, right?

01:13:51.844 --> 01:13:53.723
And they've got the dart boards
hung on the wall

01:13:54.363 --> 01:13:55.843
and you're throwing your darts at the wall.

01:13:56.343 --> 01:13:59.224
You don't know if that dart board is
hung a little high

01:13:59.603 --> 01:14:01.763
or hung a little low. You just know
you're trying to hit it.

01:14:02.384 --> 01:14:04.663
And that's what your body's doing with
expenditure and intake.

01:14:05.163 --> 01:14:08.063
It doesn't know if you happen to have a little
bit of a high metabolic rate

01:14:08.263 --> 01:14:11.263
or a little bit of a low metabolic rate. It's just trying
to balance the budget and hit the center

01:14:11.503 --> 01:14:15.644
of the bullseye, right? So, if I s-... If I came and said, "Oh,
yeah. Your, your dart boards are hung a little

01:14:15.723 --> 01:14:19.343
high," I'm still gonna find that the darts are
stuck around the, the center-

01:14:19.343 --> 01:14:19.484
Yeah.

01:14:19.484 --> 01:14:20.723
... because people are still aiming at
the center. They're not

01:14:20.783 --> 01:14:24.484
aiming at what they... At, you know, some
global middle dart board height.

01:14:24.484 --> 01:14:24.523
Yeah.

01:14:24.523 --> 01:14:26.503
They're naming it that one, and
that's how your body

01:14:26.563 --> 01:14:30.583
works. You're aiming at that metabolic rate. And so,
if you're high, you're high, that's

01:14:30.663 --> 01:14:32.903
fine. And... But, but missing the bullseye

01:14:33.603 --> 01:14:36.343
and gaining or losing a little bit of weight
is gonna be independent of

01:14:36.843 --> 01:14:39.383
if the guy next door is aiming at a
slightly lower dart board.

01:14:39.383 --> 01:14:42.883
Yeah. So, if you take the person who
says, "I eat all the time."

01:14:42.883 --> 01:14:42.983
Yeah.

01:14:42.983 --> 01:14:45.863
"I'm constantly stuffing my face. I
never gain weight."

01:14:45.863 --> 01:14:45.983
Yeah.

01:14:45.983 --> 01:14:49.523
And you contrast that with a person who feels like they're
doing the opposite, what you're saying is

01:14:50.123 --> 01:14:50.943
most likely,

01:14:51.903 --> 01:14:54.723
that energy intake for the person that feels
like they're stuffing their face

01:14:55.103 --> 01:14:56.763
is probably not as high as they think?

01:14:56.763 --> 01:15:00.643
Yes. And vice versa, the person who says that
they're not eating a lot, uh, doesn't

01:15:00.743 --> 01:15:02.663
feel that they are, probably is
eating more than they

01:15:02.723 --> 01:15:06.343
think. And that's, you know... We've known that for a
long time, that people under-report.

01:15:06.343 --> 01:15:06.603
Yeah.

01:15:06.603 --> 01:15:09.583
Sometimes they over-report, but usually
they under-report, um,

01:15:10.003 --> 01:15:13.203
and there's all sorts of obvious reasons about that.
That's not intentional. Nobody's... You know.

01:15:13.203 --> 01:15:13.223
No, no, no.

01:15:13.223 --> 01:15:14.703
I'm not saying people are lying.
That's not the point.

01:15:15.063 --> 01:15:16.923
You just... People have a hard time
tracking what they do.

01:15:16.923 --> 01:15:20.383
Yeah. I would say, in my experience, the people
who feel like they're the former,

01:15:21.103 --> 01:15:22.803
the "I can eat anything I want," they're-

01:15:22.803 --> 01:15:23.803
Yeah.

01:15:22.803 --> 01:15:26.463
... they'll... They end up going through really
long periods of not intaking food.

01:15:26.463 --> 01:15:27.163
Yeah.

01:15:27.163 --> 01:15:29.263
So, they'll be like, "Oh, yeah. I ate a
whole pizza last night."

01:15:29.823 --> 01:15:30.103
And you're like-

01:15:30.103 --> 01:15:30.183
Yeah.

01:15:30.183 --> 01:15:32.683
... "Oh, great." And then they had a
yogurt for breakfast.

01:15:32.683 --> 01:15:32.983
Right.

01:15:32.983 --> 01:15:35.203
And then they had a lunch they had, and
you're like, "Oh." So, actually-

01:15:35.203 --> 01:15:35.783
Exactly.

01:15:35.783 --> 01:15:36.243
... it... You feel-

01:15:36.243 --> 01:15:37.243
Exactly.

01:15:36.243 --> 01:15:37.503
... like you're feasting all the time-

01:15:37.503 --> 01:15:38.203
Exactly.

01:15:38.263 --> 01:15:41.683
... where the other folks are way more
consistent about their food intake.

01:15:41.683 --> 01:15:41.783
Mm-hmm.

01:15:41.783 --> 01:15:45.083
So, it feels like... And clearly,
there's more to it-

01:15:45.083 --> 01:15:45.323
Yeah.

01:15:45.323 --> 01:15:47.443
... than this. But, um, that's been my
experience of being like-

01:15:47.443 --> 01:15:47.543
Yeah.

01:15:47.543 --> 01:15:50.343
... they actually don't eat that much.
You just go through waves-

01:15:50.343 --> 01:15:51.083
Yeah.

01:15:51.083 --> 01:15:51.603
... of-

01:15:51.603 --> 01:15:52.463
Yeah. That makes sense.

01:15:52.463 --> 01:15:52.503
... eating enough.

01:15:52.503 --> 01:15:53.143
Yeah. Totally.

01:15:53.143 --> 01:15:56.403
Do people tend to be more physically
active, whether it's exercise or

01:15:57.103 --> 01:15:57.643
activity

01:15:58.403 --> 01:16:01.863
in periods of short caloric surplus? So,
if I eat a whole bunch-

01:16:01.863 --> 01:16:02.163
Oh, that's interesting.

01:16:02.163 --> 01:16:04.763
... more this weekend, am I gonna be
more active next week?

01:16:04.763 --> 01:16:05.163
Huh.

01:16:06.263 --> 01:16:07.203
I don't know

01:16:07.783 --> 01:16:08.783
any data on that.

01:16:09.643 --> 01:16:12.123
That, that's a nice prediction.
You could imagine

01:16:12.243 --> 01:16:14.723
sort of evolutionary reasons that those
mechanisms would exist.

01:16:14.723 --> 01:16:15.663
Yep.

01:16:15.663 --> 01:16:16.023
Uh,

01:16:17.443 --> 01:16:18.883
good question. Yeah. I don't know.

01:16:18.883 --> 01:16:20.723
'Cause then we don't know the
opposite, I assume, as well,

01:16:20.783 --> 01:16:23.583
right? So, if you pull calories a
little bit this week-

01:16:23.583 --> 01:16:23.863
Yeah.

01:16:23.863 --> 01:16:27.123
... uh, are you gonna be... 'Cause you hear people
say these things, right? "Man-"

01:16:27.123 --> 01:16:28.123
Yeah.

01:16:27.123 --> 01:16:30.703
"... if I pull calories down, I just...
I, uh, I don't move."

01:16:30.703 --> 01:16:35.503
That one seems, you know, the fatigue response and, you
know, and esp-... We know that if you

01:16:35.763 --> 01:16:39.343
really crash diet, your basal metabolic rate
can go down as a response.

01:16:39.343 --> 01:16:40.143
Oh, sure. Yeah.

01:16:40.143 --> 01:16:42.863
But you're not talking about... You're talking
about something smaller than that. Um,

01:16:43.703 --> 01:16:47.943
yeah. I... Gosh, I don't know. It seems like a really easy one
to measure, and maybe people have done it, but I

01:16:48.043 --> 01:16:49.943
can't think of a study that's looked at it.

