01:19:56.423 --> 01:19:59.363
interactions with environment look like,
how that interplay happens.

01:20:00.003 --> 01:20:00.403
And then,

01:20:00.963 --> 01:20:04.783
the evolutionary perspective is,
it gives you a

01:20:05.263 --> 01:20:07.683
kind of working hypothesis, a working model

01:20:08.123 --> 01:20:13.563
f- for, for expecting how the body will work, how it will
respond, what the body is trying to do.

01:20:14.303 --> 01:20:19.323
The body is not trying... You know, your, your body isn't
evolved to make you look great for the beach.

01:20:20.103 --> 01:20:22.783
Your body isn't evolved to lift... you know,

01:20:23.103 --> 01:20:27.243
to, to be a massive bodybuilder necessarily, or whatever
it is that you're trying to accomplish.

01:20:28.343 --> 01:20:30.943
W- understanding what the body's kind
of organized toward,

01:20:31.143 --> 01:20:34.143
the organizing theory of the body, which
is evolutionary success,

01:20:35.263 --> 01:20:37.943
I think helps you make sense of
the physiology. Why

01:20:38.343 --> 01:20:41.523
does my body adapt to physical activity the
way it does? Well, because

01:20:42.143 --> 01:20:45.703
we are a physically active species and have
been for two million years. Um,

01:20:46.203 --> 01:20:50.935
and so, you know-... uh, w- our entire
physiology is organized around that.

01:20:51.336 --> 01:20:57.116
You take that away, you have problems, right? So, I, I think
that's the advantage. It isn't to say, "Oh,

01:20:57.635 --> 01:21:01.255
a hunter gatherer lives this way,
therefore I'm going to

01:21:01.856 --> 01:21:03.655
cosplay like a hunter gatherer-"

01:21:03.655 --> 01:21:04.655
(laughs)

01:21:03.655 --> 01:21:05.515
"... and be healthy." I mean,
that's silly, right?

01:21:05.515 --> 01:21:05.555
Right.

01:21:05.555 --> 01:21:06.196
That's goofy.

01:21:06.196 --> 01:21:08.295
I'll give you a couple of direct examples.

01:21:08.295 --> 01:21:09.175
Yeah.

01:21:09.175 --> 01:21:13.015
I think that was great, by the way. That was
actually really helpful. When you hear

01:21:13.575 --> 01:21:17.435
justification of a particular methodology, it
doesn't matter, diet, exercise-

01:21:17.435 --> 01:21:17.475
Yeah.

01:21:17.475 --> 01:21:18.735
... psychological,

01:21:19.295 --> 01:21:20.755
uh, emot- relationships.

01:21:20.755 --> 01:21:21.115
Yeah.

01:21:21.115 --> 01:21:22.876
Like, you, you pick how humans interact.

01:21:23.175 --> 01:21:27.175
You could fall back to basically any
time point, find some

01:21:27.295 --> 01:21:29.716
previous part of our history in
which people did it-

01:21:29.716 --> 01:21:29.915
Yeah.

01:21:29.915 --> 01:21:31.135
... this way and justify it, right? This is

01:21:31.735 --> 01:21:35.455
the natural fallacy of, uh, I skipped
over. I know you know, but

01:21:36.196 --> 01:21:40.235
is the idea that just because it is natural,
then therefore that is correct.

01:21:40.235 --> 01:21:41.095
Right. Yeah.

01:21:41.095 --> 01:21:43.075
Right? And there are a lot of things
that are natural that

01:21:43.595 --> 01:21:45.115
we don't wanna do anymore, right?

01:21:45.335 --> 01:21:45.355
Yes.

01:21:45.355 --> 01:21:46.375
We don't have to list them.

01:21:46.375 --> 01:21:47.375
Yeah.

01:21:46.375 --> 01:21:47.315
But you can imagine

01:21:48.195 --> 01:21:52.355
ancient humans did some activity in a certain way that
we don't wanna do anymore as a culture.

01:21:52.355 --> 01:21:52.775
Yes.

01:21:52.775 --> 01:21:53.895
You see? That's not okay.

01:21:53.895 --> 01:21:54.515
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:21:54.515 --> 01:21:54.855
Right?

01:21:55.175 --> 01:21:57.395
Fill in the blanks, folks, if you,
if you want there.

01:21:57.395 --> 01:21:58.495
Cannibalism's a fun one.

01:21:58.495 --> 01:21:59.795
Okay, great, right?

01:21:59.795 --> 01:22:03.395
(laughs) Yeah, you can find a popula- y- there's
documented evidence in the past

01:22:03.895 --> 01:22:05.755
of people, different populations,

01:22:06.195 --> 01:22:07.775
uh, who practiced cannibalism. Now,

01:22:08.015 --> 01:22:13.575
why and how, those details are an interesting
question. But, um, nobody would advocate

01:22:14.715 --> 01:22:15.135
now

01:22:15.955 --> 01:22:17.575
that that's a great thing we oughta be doing.

01:22:17.575 --> 01:22:18.475
I'm sure somebody would.

01:22:18.475 --> 01:22:19.235
Yeah.

01:22:19.235 --> 01:22:20.355
I'm sure somebody would, right?

01:22:20.355 --> 01:22:23.255
The world's a crazy place. But most folks
are not gonna wanna do that.

01:22:23.255 --> 01:22:27.155
So, if the natural fallacy exists, then
why does it matter at all

01:22:27.835 --> 01:22:28.935
that we study all people?

01:22:28.935 --> 01:22:30.655
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah. No, I mean, again,

01:22:30.795 --> 01:22:33.535
that, that gets me back to this diversity
thing. Because if you wanna know

01:22:34.195 --> 01:22:37.875
what, uh, a really physically active day in,
day out lifestyle looks like

01:22:38.835 --> 01:22:43.595
or, uh, you know, how our bodies juggle those demands, well
then let's go and ask that question. Let's go

01:22:43.715 --> 01:22:45.495
find populations that are really
physically active.

01:22:45.715 --> 01:22:48.235
Let's w- find ones that are sedentary. Let's
find ones that eat a really

01:22:48.355 --> 01:22:51.355
high carb diet. Let's find ones that eat a
really low, you know, high

01:22:51.595 --> 01:22:56.055
meat diet. Um, and that's a way of asking about
that diversity. I think you also

01:22:56.135 --> 01:22:59.475
learn some other interesting things. You learn
about local adaptations in some cases

01:22:59.875 --> 01:23:02.155
that you otherwise would, would miss. Um,

01:23:02.775 --> 01:23:06.175
but, uh, but I, I think that's the
advantage is, um,

01:23:06.635 --> 01:23:09.335
in terms of, in terms of when we br- wanna
bring it back home to a health

01:23:09.995 --> 01:23:10.995
context, right?

01:23:10.995 --> 01:23:11.115
Yeah.

01:23:11.115 --> 01:23:13.295
What we're gonna learn about how we're
gonna fix our own lives today.

01:23:13.895 --> 01:23:15.215
That's it. You're learning about

01:23:15.855 --> 01:23:18.815
these sort of, th- the diversity of human
experience. And you can pick and

01:23:18.915 --> 01:23:22.475
choose the lessons from that, not in a
lateralistic fallacy kinda way, but in a,

01:23:22.715 --> 01:23:24.955
hey, they've tried it that way for...
and, and this is how

01:23:25.015 --> 01:23:30.595
they result. Th- this is how their body results from that.
I can bring that home. Great example. Um,

01:23:31.535 --> 01:23:36.855
physical activity we know is important, uh, for lots of
reasons. But like, how much should you get?

01:23:37.555 --> 01:23:39.075
Right? That's a fair question to ask.

01:23:39.075 --> 01:23:39.755
Mm-hmm.

01:23:39.755 --> 01:23:45.275
And it turns out that if you, um, look at how much,
uh... This is just a fun little comparison.

01:23:45.555 --> 01:23:48.335
If you look at how much, uh, physical
activity folks get, um,

01:23:48.735 --> 01:23:50.195
in a, a nice study that was done in, in

01:23:50.375 --> 01:23:55.015
Scotland. Uh, it's, uh, where was it?
Glasgow. It's, it's gl- in Glasgow.

01:23:55.455 --> 01:23:58.915
These Glaswegian mail carriers... All
right, this is a fun one.

01:23:59.435 --> 01:24:01.995
Uh, you have folks who are carrying
the mail every day

01:24:02.135 --> 01:24:04.555
getting 17,000 steps a day. You have
folks who are in the

01:24:04.635 --> 01:24:06.515
office sorting mail, and they're not
getting the steps.

01:24:06.995 --> 01:24:09.615
And you ask, "Who has the best health
outcomes?" And it's all National Health

01:24:09.715 --> 01:24:12.795
Services up in the UK, so it's all kind
of controlled that way.

01:24:13.315 --> 01:24:15.315
The real big difference here is
how active you are.

01:24:15.955 --> 01:24:18.875
The folks who are getting the s- the
17,000 steps a day

01:24:19.315 --> 01:24:23.055
are, turns out, really well protected
against cardiovascular disease-

01:24:23.055 --> 01:24:23.175
Yeah.

01:24:23.175 --> 01:24:23.735
... and all these things.

01:24:24.655 --> 01:24:24.855
Well,

01:24:25.675 --> 01:24:27.615
how d- how do we understand that? Well,
it turns out, if you look

01:24:27.675 --> 01:24:30.935
at, you know, traditional groups around the
world, that's kind of the

01:24:31.015 --> 01:24:33.775
sweet spot of where people are getting their
activity. That's how much they're getting

01:24:34.355 --> 01:24:37.855
in a hunting and gathering context or a
farming context. It matches up.

01:24:38.335 --> 01:24:40.395
And so, that's, you know... Not that those

01:24:40.495 --> 01:24:43.735
mail carriers were thinking, "Gosh, I wanna
make sure I get as many"-

01:24:43.735 --> 01:24:43.815
Sure.

01:24:43.815 --> 01:24:45.115
... "steps as a hunter gatherer today."

01:24:45.835 --> 01:24:47.475
Th- that's where we can learn something from

01:24:47.815 --> 01:24:53.535
another cultural context, you know, a non-industrialized
context, and, and it helps make sense of

01:24:54.115 --> 01:24:56.915
the practical things we can do in our own
lives here. We don't have to act

01:24:57.215 --> 01:24:59.875
like hunter gatherers. That's not the goal.
The goal is to, to learn.

01:24:59.875 --> 01:25:00.615
Yeah.

01:25:01.175 --> 01:25:03.515
You and I are gonna focus on physiology-

01:25:03.515 --> 01:25:03.615
Yeah.

01:25:03.615 --> 01:25:05.495
... metabolism and exercise.

01:25:05.495 --> 01:25:05.715
Mm-hmm.

01:25:05.715 --> 01:25:05.835
Great. But

01:25:06.715 --> 01:25:08.995
you can find these same examples in

01:25:09.895 --> 01:25:10.895
cultural-

01:25:10.895 --> 01:25:11.895
Yeah.

01:25:10.895 --> 01:25:13.735
... in relationship, in how people
interac-... Like-

01:25:13.735 --> 01:25:13.815
Yeah.

01:25:13.815 --> 01:25:17.035
... we're just gonna focus on this aspect of it. But I
really think it's important that people

01:25:17.395 --> 01:25:21.075
extend that thinking out to this is all of
how we behave as a species-

01:25:21.075 --> 01:25:21.315
Yeah.

01:25:21.315 --> 01:25:24.155
... that can go there. So, with the,

01:25:24.935 --> 01:25:29.495
uh... I don't know if that was stuff in the UK Biobank,
but w- in the Glasgow, uh, example there-

01:25:29.495 --> 01:25:29.655
Yeah.

01:25:29.655 --> 01:25:31.535
... you're highlighting another

01:25:32.515 --> 01:25:35.255
positive role of physical
activity and exercise-

01:25:35.255 --> 01:25:35.495
Mm-hmm.

01:25:35.495 --> 01:25:38.635
... but just not from the
caloric expenditure.

01:25:38.635 --> 01:25:38.835
Yeah.

01:25:38.835 --> 01:25:40.435
Right? That... I mean, that's kind of
what you're saying is-

01:25:40.495 --> 01:25:41.495
Yeah.

01:25:40.495 --> 01:25:42.835
... you're not denying and suggesting
that so many people

01:25:43.515 --> 01:25:44.935
and so many (laughs) magazines-

01:25:44.935 --> 01:25:45.935
Yeah.

01:25:44.935 --> 01:25:46.615
... and articles have said that
you're saying.

01:25:47.755 --> 01:25:50.075
There are plenty of physical activity
benefits, it's just-

01:25:50.075 --> 01:25:50.095
100%.

01:25:50.095 --> 01:25:53.175
... perhaps not from the caloric
expenditure side of the equation.

01:25:53.175 --> 01:25:54.095
That's right.

01:25:54.095 --> 01:25:57.355
Right, right. So, I just want to triple,
quadruple down on what you don't,

01:25:57.975 --> 01:25:58.575
don't say.

01:25:58.575 --> 01:26:00.055
(laughs) Right.

01:26:00.055 --> 01:26:02.575
But getting back to this. How do we then

01:26:03.595 --> 01:26:05.875
decide which of these learnings

01:26:06.815 --> 01:26:08.615
from anthropology-

01:26:08.615 --> 01:26:09.615
Mm-hmm.

01:26:08.615 --> 01:26:13.715
... that we choose to take, like physical activity, but then
we don't choose to take the cannibalism one?

01:26:13.715 --> 01:26:14.015
Right.

01:26:14.855 --> 01:26:18.715
Well, uh, that gets to this, uh, question of,
you know, biology can tell us

01:26:18.955 --> 01:26:23.135
how things work. Cultural studies can tell us the
diversity of what possibility spaces are.

01:26:23.635 --> 01:26:29.535
You know, but then it, it all ends up... You still have
the hard decision to make yourself as in

01:26:30.215 --> 01:26:32.175
what, what are my principles-

01:26:32.175 --> 01:26:32.235
Yeah.

01:26:32.235 --> 01:26:35.275
... what are my, uh, you know, guiding,

01:26:35.355 --> 01:26:38.075
uh, ideas, and, and how do I bring
this into my own life?

01:26:38.155 --> 01:26:41.615
So, um, that's why you're probably not
gonna bring cannibalism in.

01:26:41.615 --> 01:26:42.375
(laughs)

01:26:42.375 --> 01:26:43.395
But you might wanna exercise.

01:26:43.395 --> 01:26:43.895
Speak for yourself.

01:26:43.895 --> 01:26:49.275
Yeah, fine. Uh, you know, and I thi- I think there's also a
broader importance here, and that is I think

01:26:50.055 --> 01:26:51.015
we reduce

01:26:51.555 --> 01:26:54.395
a lot of how we study the natural
world and study ourselves

01:26:55.015 --> 01:26:55.675
down to

01:26:56.495 --> 01:27:00.415
what's the next thing I'm gonna do to, to, to
tweak my life, to give myself

01:27:00.675 --> 01:27:03.775
more energy, better health, whatever. Those are all
important things to have. We should all want

01:27:04.015 --> 01:27:06.435
to maximize our time here, absolutely.

01:27:07.535 --> 01:27:07.735
But

01:27:08.495 --> 01:27:12.015
there's a broader question, which is, how
do we understand the world?

01:27:12.335 --> 01:27:14.535
How do we understand ourselves? How
do we understand

01:27:15.275 --> 01:27:16.275
everyone around us?

01:27:16.815 --> 01:27:19.215
Um, and you're not gonna get

01:27:19.775 --> 01:27:25.575
all those answers with blinders on, narrowly focused on your
neighborhood and your immediate ar- area.

01:27:25.575 --> 01:27:26.095
Mm-hmm.

01:27:26.095 --> 01:27:29.035
You're gonna get those answers, I would
argue, by casting a pretty

01:27:29.115 --> 01:27:33.955
broad net, asking what's going on around the world,
even in different species. But, you know,

01:27:34.395 --> 01:27:37.635
let's keep it to our species for a moment,
asking those questions about

01:27:37.715 --> 01:27:43.335
what happens in different cultures and different contexts.
And, and I think there's a richness there, um,

01:27:43.875 --> 01:27:48.155
that actually does improve our lives in ways
that are harder to measure. Um,

01:27:48.955 --> 01:27:51.335
but that, you know, uh, we often
wanna reduce things down

01:27:51.395 --> 01:27:54.235
to the bottom line, to the health
nugget, to the biohack.

01:27:54.235 --> 01:27:55.055
Yeah.

01:27:55.055 --> 01:27:58.615
And, okay, I get it. But there's a broader thing
here that I think is valuable.

01:27:58.615 --> 01:27:59.635
Right.

01:28:00.335 --> 01:28:02.595
Very... Could not agree more. Really well

01:28:03.195 --> 01:28:06.615
said there. How much do we know
about the variation?

01:28:06.875 --> 01:28:10.855
And let's just stay with our wheelhouse here, of
something like energy expenditure-

01:28:10.855 --> 01:28:11.855
Mm-hmm.

01:28:10.855 --> 01:28:12.115
... among other,

01:28:13.215 --> 01:28:18.255
uh, older forms of lifestyle, if you will. So,
clearly you've spent time in Africa-

01:28:18.255 --> 01:28:19.255
Mm-hmm.

01:28:18.255 --> 01:28:20.295
... and I don't know if you've gone
to Central America.

01:28:20.775 --> 01:28:23.055
But how much variation do we know
exists between

01:28:23.175 --> 01:28:26.915
Southeast Asia cultures, European,
Scandinavian, and other ones?

01:28:27.475 --> 01:28:31.355
I assume you think your findings with the
Hadza group would extend to

01:28:32.315 --> 01:28:34.335
everyone else. But what do we
actually know, if any-

01:28:34.335 --> 01:28:34.375
Yeah.

01:28:34.375 --> 01:28:35.155
... about other groups?

01:28:35.155 --> 01:28:38.035
Great question. So, now we have this big, uh,

01:28:38.195 --> 01:28:39.995
global database that we're putting together

01:28:40.055 --> 01:28:43.695
has allowed us to kinda ask that question, which is
really fun. And so, we've got, um...

01:28:44.155 --> 01:28:47.555
In the latest iteration of it, I think we've got
35 populations from around the

01:28:47.655 --> 01:45:05.371
world.But between those genes are lots of stretches of DNA
that, as far as we can tell, don't do anything.

01:45:06.692 --> 01:45:11.691
And because of that, you don't have evolutionary pressures
keeping them the same. They tend to vary more.

01:45:12.871 --> 01:45:16.051
And they var- vary in a random way,
and so they create

01:45:16.172 --> 01:45:20.431
noise, let's say, in your genome. Now, the
random processes that create

01:45:20.552 --> 01:45:22.972
noises in one population are
unlikely to be the same

01:45:23.792 --> 01:45:27.751
as they happen in another population. So
those random, meaningless

01:45:27.991 --> 01:45:31.311
bits of noise in your genome actually
become a nice fingerprint-

01:45:31.311 --> 01:45:31.511
Mm-hmm.

01:45:31.511 --> 01:45:33.571
... for where your recent ancestry is.

01:45:34.152 --> 01:45:38.472
Because the same random things that happened in
Scandinavia probably didn't happen

01:45:39.111 --> 01:45:40.051
in Southeast Asia.

01:45:40.711 --> 01:45:41.312
And so,

01:45:41.931 --> 01:45:45.271
that's what a company, a retail genetics
company, is doing. They're looking

01:45:45.511 --> 01:45:48.831
for those kind of meaningless... Actually, it...
The more meaningless, the better.

01:45:48.831 --> 01:45:50.051
Yeah, the noncoding stuff, right? Yeah.

01:45:50.051 --> 01:45:51.731
The noncoding stuff. And so, um,

01:45:52.611 --> 01:45:57.132
yeah, so that's funny because then I think it... Those tests
are, I think, are really fun to play with.

01:45:57.911 --> 01:46:02.551
They give you the sense that those genetic differences
are big and meaningful when, in fact, the

01:46:02.731 --> 01:46:05.451
reason they work is that they're not
meaningful (laughs)

01:46:05.451 --> 01:46:07.331
By definition.

01:46:07.331 --> 01:46:07.531
Yeah.

01:46:07.531 --> 01:46:09.531
We don't yet know what... Uh, that
they're doing anything.

01:46:09.531 --> 01:46:11.791
Exactly. Yeah. That's right.

01:46:11.791 --> 01:46:16.051
How do we distinguish, though? How do we draw that line?
How... When you said recent ancestors.

01:46:16.051 --> 01:46:16.291
Hmm.

01:46:16.951 --> 01:46:23.811
Well, that's another thing. So we know now, uh, that, uh,
you know, people have been mixing and moving

01:46:24.391 --> 01:46:27.571
globally forever. So there are no, uh,

01:46:27.911 --> 01:46:31.431
you know, sort of genetically isolated
pure populations, and,

01:46:31.651 --> 01:46:34.371
uh, everybody intermixes with everybody.
That is just the truth.

01:46:35.151 --> 01:46:35.411
Um,

01:46:35.991 --> 01:46:36.591
and so

01:46:37.831 --> 01:46:42.831
I couldn't use, for example, I would have a hard time... Uh,
let, let's say it this way, those little, uh,

01:46:42.971 --> 01:46:45.711
random noise signals that tell me
I'm from Scandinavia

01:46:45.871 --> 01:46:50.411
or s- or Southeast Asia, those only work 'cause recently
we, we measured a bunch of people from those

01:46:50.511 --> 01:46:52.531
places, and we know what that noise
looks like there.

01:46:52.531 --> 01:46:53.031
Right.

01:46:53.031 --> 01:46:55.871
But check back in in 100 years, and it might be
different, right? 'Cause it's gonna keep

01:46:55.931 --> 01:47:00.391
on churning. This, this churn is gonna keep on happening.
It's been happening for 20... It's... For

01:47:00.831 --> 01:47:04.511
200,000 years, and that's gonna keep on
sort of mixing it up.

01:47:04.511 --> 01:47:07.151
How good would you

01:47:08.291 --> 01:47:09.731
call our current

01:47:10.271 --> 01:47:10.951
database

01:47:11.671 --> 01:47:13.191
of genetics across the-

01:47:13.191 --> 01:47:13.331
Mm.

01:47:13.331 --> 01:47:14.911
... the variety of cultures?

01:47:14.911 --> 01:47:19.571
We know it's hugely uneven, right? Uh, the

01:47:20.091 --> 01:47:22.631
most data we have in terms of, you know,

01:47:23.111 --> 01:47:25.271
UK Biobank or GenBank or any of these

01:47:25.411 --> 01:47:29.491
big databases, or for that matter, the retail
space of, uh, you know, the, the-

01:47:29.491 --> 01:47:30.031
Yeah.

01:47:30.031 --> 01:47:31.511
Those are all, um,

01:47:32.331 --> 01:47:33.911
wealthy, largely white

01:47:34.691 --> 01:47:37.591
populations and countries. Not entirely.
There's, you know, there's

01:47:38.051 --> 01:47:41.631
fair... The people work very hard to get coverage as
far as everywhere they can get it.

01:47:42.091 --> 01:47:44.011
But just in terms of the bulk of
the numbers, yeah.

01:47:44.011 --> 01:47:48.431
I think the more recent data suggests that still,
to this point, 90-plus percent-

01:47:48.431 --> 01:47:49.211
Yeah.

01:47:49.211 --> 01:47:50.371
... of genetic data is on

01:47:51.491 --> 01:47:52.191
white Caucasian.

01:47:52.191 --> 01:47:53.971
That's right. That's right. And what we know

01:47:54.371 --> 01:47:58.871
is that when we go... We can talk about this next.
When we do a study of, uh, you know,

01:47:58.991 --> 01:48:01.131
what, what gene variants predict height-

01:48:01.131 --> 01:48:02.671
I, I would love for you to go into
this. Yes, please do.

01:48:02.671 --> 01:48:04.751
Yeah. What gene variants predict height?

01:48:05.351 --> 01:48:07.731
What gene variants predict
cardiovascular risk,

01:48:08.431 --> 01:48:10.371
disease risk? What gene variants predict

01:48:10.591 --> 01:48:15.831
an... Pick your trait. When those studies are done in
the UK Biobank with a largely white

01:48:16.331 --> 01:48:19.251
population, it turns out they're not
what they call portable.

01:48:20.051 --> 01:48:25.331
I can find those same gene variants anywhere else because,
