01:48:25.831 --> 01:48:27.751
But it turns out they're not
predictive over there anymore.

01:48:27.751 --> 01:48:27.911
Totally.

01:48:27.911 --> 01:48:28.291
Oops.

01:48:29.331 --> 01:48:30.791
Right? And so, um,

01:48:31.631 --> 01:48:33.871
yeah. It's a, it's an interesting time to
be alive, right? Because

01:48:33.991 --> 01:48:38.191
I remember in the '90s as an undergraduate, the
human genome hadn't been published yet.

01:48:38.191 --> 01:48:39.231
Right.

01:48:39.231 --> 01:48:40.491
We were in the

01:48:41.131 --> 01:48:44.931
kind of the late stages of this
reckoning about, uh,

01:48:46.191 --> 01:48:49.851
racial diversity and the fact that race is
a, is a cultural construct

01:48:50.691 --> 01:48:54.591
that has, of course, important biology, uh,
embedded in it, and, and actually

01:48:55.091 --> 01:48:58.451
racism itself can create biologies. I mean,
so there's a whole... But

01:48:58.631 --> 01:49:00.911
we understood that, that race is
not a biological

01:49:01.491 --> 01:49:03.171
category, it's a cultural category.

01:49:03.991 --> 01:49:08.371
We understood, uh, it was a reckoning about sex differences,
it was a reckoning of all these things. And

01:49:08.971 --> 01:49:10.571
we were ready, I think we were poised

01:49:10.751 --> 01:49:12.311
to do the next thing, which is to get down

01:49:12.391 --> 01:49:16.051
to the genetic level, and then we had the human
genome published, and we're still

01:49:17.071 --> 01:49:18.231
not... We haven't... It hasn't... That

01:49:18.311 --> 01:49:21.731
pro- it's a promise unfulfilled because the
gene, the genome work has gone

01:49:22.351 --> 01:49:23.651
in a kind of a different direction.

01:49:24.211 --> 01:49:28.771
Um, I think we still don't really reckon with diversity in
the ways that, that we probably ought to.

01:49:28.771 --> 01:49:31.711
Well, you could explain a lot of
things here, but

01:49:32.791 --> 01:49:34.051
one thing of note

01:49:35.211 --> 01:49:39.811
is I don't think many people have really gotten past,
or the awareness has gotten out about

01:49:40.031 --> 01:49:41.871
what I call the one-to-one gene fallacy.

01:49:41.871 --> 01:49:42.591
Mm.

01:49:42.591 --> 01:49:46.771
Which is an entire human genome, the promise
of it, the direct or indirect-

01:49:46.771 --> 01:49:47.031
Yeah.

01:49:47.031 --> 01:49:49.511
... was that, "Well, we'll sequence
every human gene-"

01:49:49.511 --> 01:49:49.811
Yeah.

01:49:49.811 --> 01:49:52.911
"... and because one gene equals
one physical trait-"

01:49:52.911 --> 01:49:53.471
Yes.

01:49:53.471 --> 01:49:54.831
"... we'll then know everything about you."

01:49:54.831 --> 01:49:59.731
That's exactly right. Yes. That's a great way to go with it,
too, because the promise was, "I'm gonna n-

01:49:59.851 --> 01:50:02.671
be able to sequence your genome, and I'm gonna
know everything about you."

01:50:03.531 --> 01:50:06.231
And it didn't happen. And now we know
that it will never happen.

01:50:06.971 --> 01:50:07.211
It won't happen.

01:50:07.211 --> 01:50:08.891
The numbers are astronomical.

01:50:08.891 --> 01:50:10.391
They're astronomical, and

01:50:11.231 --> 01:50:13.431
environment has, has too big of a role.

01:50:13.431 --> 01:50:13.731
Right.

01:50:13.731 --> 01:50:15.751
You know? In a lot of traits, it has a

01:50:16.331 --> 01:50:18.871
very dominant role, and in every
trait, it has some role.

01:50:19.531 --> 01:50:20.731
And so, uh,

01:50:21.771 --> 01:50:25.171
it's, it's... You know, it isn't that
you have three genes

01:50:25.651 --> 01:50:28.071
and the versions you have of those
determine how h- how tall you get.

01:50:28.751 --> 01:50:31.571
There are at least 10,000 genes
for which we know

01:50:32.111 --> 01:50:35.671
if you have variant A or variant B, you're gonna be a
little bit taller, a little bit shorter.

01:50:35.831 --> 01:50:38.131
And you add up all those over 10,000, and I

01:50:38.291 --> 01:50:40.371
might know something about your height, but then I
also have to know your environment

01:50:40.431 --> 01:50:45.611
'cause I only know about half the story with the
genetics, right? So it's a complicated system.

01:50:46.691 --> 01:50:51.251
Well, if we wanted to be predictive about things,
it's... We can understand it at a kind of

01:50:51.731 --> 01:50:55.571
broader level and just say, "Look, we have to understand
the interplay of environment and genetics

01:50:56.211 --> 01:50:57.551
for each trait individually,

01:50:58.111 --> 01:50:58.871
how they work."

01:50:59.511 --> 01:51:02.731
That's, that's the better way in to
understanding why your

01:51:03.091 --> 01:51:08.011
physiology is different than mine, or why this
athlete is, you know, struggling with this

01:51:08.251 --> 01:51:11.351
thing, or this person is struggling with that
disease risk, or whatever it is.

01:51:12.111 --> 01:51:15.011
When we un- wanna understand our
diversity, we have to break it

01:51:15.091 --> 01:51:20.291
down, I think, back to that kind of fundamental
beginning, and then it's a good way into it.

01:51:20.291 --> 01:51:21.651
Why does this stuff even help?

01:51:22.611 --> 01:51:24.011
Why does it even matter-

01:51:24.011 --> 01:51:24.231
Yeah.

01:51:24.231 --> 01:51:25.051
... that

01:51:25.791 --> 01:51:32.211
we're thinking about genetics, as an example, wrong? We gave
the example earlier of why it mattered for GFR.

01:51:32.211 --> 01:51:32.771
Mm-hmm.

01:51:32.771 --> 01:51:32.971
But

01:51:33.991 --> 01:51:35.951
can you think of any exa- examples of where

01:51:36.831 --> 01:51:39.931
over-generalizing by population,
or perhaps maybe

01:51:41.051 --> 01:51:45.411
not thinking about the uniqueness of that
individual population appropriately-

01:51:45.411 --> 01:51:45.611
Yeah.

01:51:45.611 --> 01:51:49.111
... matters. Like, what are the practical
implications of these mistakes-

01:51:49.111 --> 01:51:49.151
Oh.

01:51:49.151 --> 01:51:50.991
... outside of sociocultural, like...

01:51:50.991 --> 01:51:54.451
Uh, here's a fun example, and we can do a couple
of these, but here's a fun one.

01:51:54.811 --> 01:51:58.851
Uh, I was working in Northern Kenya, uh, when we first
started working on, on that project

01:51:58.971 --> 01:52:02.471
with a community, uh, called the
Dosniq, which is a traditio-

01:52:02.471 --> 01:52:03.291
Oh, perfect. Great.

01:52:03.291 --> 01:52:03.491
Yeah.

01:52:03.491 --> 01:52:04.371
Great. Let's, yes.

01:52:04.371 --> 01:52:07.391
Traditional, uh, pa- pastoralists, these folks,
you know, they live with their

01:52:07.471 --> 01:52:10.131
herds, goats and cattle and, and camels. Um,

01:52:10.291 --> 01:52:12.211
and we started this project. We, we got in

01:52:12.291 --> 01:52:16.191
the community, and of course, this is community-based
work. We, we met with, uh, community leaders.

01:52:16.731 --> 01:52:19.531
We met with the health clinic folks. We wanted
to, before we started, to make

01:52:19.591 --> 01:52:22.811
sure we were talking to everybody and getting everybody on
board, and this is something we all wanted to do.

01:52:23.951 --> 01:52:30.191
One of the folks we talked to was this, uh, German
charity that was there to try to, um,

01:52:30.571 --> 01:52:34.851
to, to, you know, reduce malnutrition in this group. And
so they're giving food supplementation

01:52:35.271 --> 01:52:39.191
to moms with little kids to try to fix what
they saw as malnutrition.

01:52:39.851 --> 01:52:41.951
Um, and so we, um,

01:52:42.671 --> 01:52:45.351
we met with this guy from the German
charity, me and, and our, my

01:52:45.431 --> 01:52:49.811
Kenyan colleague, Rosemary, who's wonderful. And, you
know, this guy's like, "Look, man,

01:52:50.411 --> 01:52:51.231
it's really bad.

01:52:52.031 --> 01:52:54.011
Malnutrition. 60% of the kids here

01:52:54.571 --> 01:52:56.651
are, are malnourished." That's scary.

01:52:57.231 --> 01:52:58.211
Obviously, that's a tragedy.

01:52:58.891 --> 01:53:01.431
And so we thought, "Oh, gosh, okay,
that's important to know."

01:53:01.891 --> 01:53:06.868
And then, um-... but you go out into the community, you go
around the villages, and that's not what you see.

01:53:07.287 --> 01:53:12.687
What you see is kids running around, playing, happy,
laughing. And if there's- if malnourished,

01:53:13.007 --> 01:53:16.868
y- you know, if- if undernourishment is that big of an issue,
you should also see issues with fertility.

01:53:16.868 --> 01:53:17.047
Mm-hmm.

01:53:17.047 --> 01:53:21.828
You don't see that. You see big families. Um, folks
are active, healthy, doing their thing.

01:53:22.447 --> 01:53:24.947
That's strange. How- how do we square
the circle on that?

01:53:25.547 --> 01:53:27.267
And it turns out, um,

01:53:27.887 --> 01:53:30.527
you know, we, uh, were talking to the
health clinic about this.

01:53:31.328 --> 01:53:34.967
And they had, in their records, partly d- with this
charity, they- they had been doing this.

01:53:35.227 --> 01:53:38.047
They had been collecting heights and weights
for children in that, uh,

01:53:38.187 --> 01:53:42.088
community, the Dostonic community, for years and
years. And they had, uh, you know,

01:53:42.207 --> 01:53:45.367
hundreds, I think a couple thousand of
measurements of- of- of

01:53:45.547 --> 01:53:48.928
kids' heights and weights from birth up through,
you know, up through childhood.

01:53:48.928 --> 01:53:49.847
Science gold.

01:53:49.847 --> 01:53:51.127
Wonderful. It's so awesome, right?

01:53:51.127 --> 01:53:52.167
Yeah. Everything you could ever hope for.

01:53:52.167 --> 01:53:56.007
Yeah. So what we were able to do is
basically make a Dostonic community

01:53:56.707 --> 01:54:00.687
growth curve, right? What the Germans
guys had been doing

01:54:00.768 --> 01:54:04.807
is, they'd been taking heights and weights of kids and
they'd been plotting them against WHO standards.

01:54:04.807 --> 01:54:06.427
Oh, there you go. There you go.

01:54:06.427 --> 01:54:06.727
Right?

01:54:06.727 --> 01:54:07.307
Yep.

01:54:07.307 --> 01:54:11.307
And the assumption there is that kids everywhere across
the globe all grow exactly the same.

01:54:11.307 --> 01:54:11.667
Bingo.

01:54:11.667 --> 01:54:14.367
The kids in the Dostonic community

01:54:15.067 --> 01:54:19.587
were doing a really cool thing, actually. They would
start off normal height and weight.

01:54:20.287 --> 01:54:22.227
They would kind of fall off the growth
curve a little bit,

01:54:22.347 --> 01:54:25.507
eh, around year one. But then around year two,
their heights would shoot up.

01:54:25.927 --> 01:54:28.487
They were taller than the a- global
standard, actually.

01:54:29.527 --> 01:54:32.867
But because of that, their height for their
weight, that ratio looked

01:54:33.387 --> 01:54:33.787
thin.

01:54:33.787 --> 01:54:34.467
Way off, yeah.

01:54:34.987 --> 01:54:36.707
Did they physically look ill? Did
they physically-

01:54:36.707 --> 01:54:38.307
They don't look, they don't look
ill, but even-

01:54:38.307 --> 01:54:38.347
Just lanky.

01:54:38.347 --> 01:54:42.127
... the folks, they just tall and thin. It's just, it's
just the body build in that population.

01:54:42.787 --> 01:54:45.047
And so, um, the German charity,

01:54:45.687 --> 01:54:47.127
where they had their hearts in the right

01:54:47.247 --> 01:54:53.647
place, they wanted to help, but you are never gonna get that
popula- those kids to look like, you know,

01:54:54.107 --> 01:54:59.527
WHO standard kids. 'Cause they are in- in that
population, and that's, you know,

01:54:59.927 --> 01:55:03.527
the alleles that- that- that promote a tall,
thin build are really common.

01:55:04.727 --> 01:55:06.927
That's an adaptation in that part
of the world for

01:55:07.287 --> 01:55:10.767
dealing with really hot climates is it's incredibly
hot there. You're on the equator.

01:55:11.787 --> 01:55:14.267
Um, being tall and thin is a way to- to

01:55:14.447 --> 01:55:17.787
be able to thermoregulate better. That's been known for a
long time. We see that a- around the world.

01:55:18.327 --> 01:55:22.627
Tall, thin body build tends to be more common in really hot
climates 'cause it's a way to dump heat.

01:55:22.627 --> 01:55:23.687
Yeah, surface area, along, right?

01:55:23.687 --> 01:55:24.587
Surface area, exactly. Yep.

01:55:24.587 --> 01:55:24.767
Yeah.

01:55:24.767 --> 01:55:28.847
Um, and so these kids were doing exactly what
their bodies were built to do.

01:55:29.147 --> 01:55:33.107
Now, if you understand the way that height
works, just to pick a trait, and the

01:55:33.167 --> 01:55:35.047
interplay of genetics and environment,

01:55:35.887 --> 01:55:36.227
um,

01:55:37.127 --> 01:55:40.467
then you can look at that and say, "Oh, that's the
system. Now I understand it." Now,

01:55:40.547 --> 01:55:42.147
actually, now that you have a
Dostonic-specific

01:55:42.227 --> 01:55:44.607
growth curve, now you could pick the kids
that really do need help.

01:55:44.607 --> 01:55:45.347
Oh, yeah.

01:55:45.347 --> 01:55:48.867
That's great. You could help them. You could
focus the help better. Um, and

01:55:48.947 --> 01:55:50.747
I don't fault the German charity
for wanting to help.

01:55:50.747 --> 01:55:51.427
Clearly.

01:55:51.427 --> 01:55:54.867
But they were never gonna achieve. They
actually left. They- they

01:55:55.027 --> 01:55:58.027
closed up shop and left because they thought, "We
