... you're supposed to tell them how many Hispanic,
non-white His- n- non-black Hispanic, uh,

01:58:50.267 --> 01:58:54.087
non-Hispanic whites, b- l- this is the
census categories, right?

01:58:54.087 --> 01:58:54.447
Yep.

01:58:54.447 --> 01:58:59.267
And then somehow those categories are used to interpret
results of your population-based data

01:59:00.067 --> 01:59:01.747
in a way that we know

01:59:02.707 --> 01:59:05.087
going into it is fundamentally
problematic. And, you know,

01:59:06.307 --> 01:59:08.887
there's... (laughs) I'm not saying that we
don't wanna have diverse

01:59:08.967 --> 01:59:10.547
cohorts. I'm saying the opposite, actually.

01:59:10.547 --> 01:59:11.727
I- I... Okay, thank you.

01:59:11.727 --> 01:59:11.907
Yeah.

01:59:11.907 --> 01:59:12.287
Yes.

01:59:12.287 --> 01:59:14.827
I'm saying the opposite. I'm saying that let's
treat, you know, let- let's

01:59:14.927 --> 01:59:16.287
take these individual by individual.

01:59:16.927 --> 01:59:21.307
If, um... And let's look at
trait-specific, uh,

01:59:21.847 --> 01:59:26.667
for, you know, let's look at trait-specific physiology. Let's
look at those individual axes of variation.

01:59:27.247 --> 01:59:27.487
Um,

01:59:28.087 --> 01:59:29.387
it might be helpful to know

01:59:30.007 --> 01:59:35.307
in your big population that folks who are Black in the
United States have these physiological outcomes.

01:59:35.307 --> 01:59:35.607
Yeah.

01:59:35.607 --> 01:59:37.927
But that's not telling you that
skin color does that

01:59:38.547 --> 01:59:42.227
or that their genetics did that. That's
flagging that perhaps

01:59:42.667 --> 01:59:46.147
their environment, because of, you know, all the
cultural baggage we have in the US,

01:59:46.607 --> 01:59:49.467
are affecting physiologies that way. Let's,
let's understand it that way.

01:59:49.987 --> 01:59:50.547
Um, so

01:59:51.287 --> 01:59:55.047
I'm not... We, we need diverse research. We need
diverse research populations. But the way

01:59:55.107 --> 01:59:56.707
that we handle it just has to be different.

01:59:56.707 --> 01:59:59.287
Yeah. I actually... I love how
you frame that.

01:59:59.807 --> 02:00:03.387
If I could throw a, uh, a little bit of an
analogy on the spot here on that-

02:00:03.387 --> 02:00:03.447
Yeah.

02:00:03.447 --> 02:00:05.307
... the way that I generally like
to think about

02:00:05.847 --> 02:00:08.787
population-based data, and let's just keep
using that example. One

02:00:08.987 --> 02:00:14.267
trait that you'll see pop up, we said earlier,
um, for Black, African American-

02:00:14.267 --> 02:00:14.547
Mm-hmm.

02:00:14.547 --> 02:00:17.867
... much higher rates of type 2 diabetes.

02:00:17.867 --> 02:00:18.687
Mm-hmm.

02:00:18.687 --> 02:00:21.407
Much, generally higher rates of
obesity, right?

02:00:21.927 --> 02:00:25.467
So what you're saying is population data
can shine a light on that.

02:00:25.467 --> 02:00:26.087
Yeah.

02:00:26.087 --> 02:00:31.807
And that allows us to say, "Hey, we need more focus. We
need more emphasis. We need more resources."

02:00:31.807 --> 02:00:32.227
Yeah.

02:00:32.227 --> 02:00:32.887
Does that

02:00:33.687 --> 02:00:38.047
then tell you, if you're Black, there's something in
your physiology that makes you, uh,

02:00:38.767 --> 02:00:43.427
have more diabetes? Maybe, maybe not. All it's simply saying
is there's something in the entire picture.

02:00:43.427 --> 02:00:49.147
Yeah. That's a great example. An- another one, uh, is heart
disease, hypertension and heart disease.

02:00:49.147 --> 02:00:49.307
Yep.

02:00:49.307 --> 02:00:55.007
We know that, uh, Black folks, Native Americans, other
minorities have higher rates of hypertension

02:00:55.547 --> 02:00:59.807
and heart disease, uh, than whites in the
US. That's... We know that.

02:00:59.807 --> 02:01:00.247
Yeah.

02:01:00.247 --> 02:01:03.087
For a long time, in the '80s, '90s, uh,

02:01:03.327 --> 02:01:08.907
particularly the observation that we had higher rates of
hypertension among Black Americans was taken to

02:01:09.187 --> 02:01:12.959
somehow mean-... interpreted it as, they
have a genetic predisposition

02:01:13.659 --> 02:01:14.819
to hypertension.

02:01:14.819 --> 02:01:16.939
I spent my graduate work in Memphis-

02:01:16.939 --> 02:01:17.259
Yeah.

02:01:17.259 --> 02:01:19.179
... in health science. I had to teach this.

02:01:19.179 --> 02:01:20.139
And it's wrong.

02:01:20.139 --> 02:01:20.440
Yeah.

02:01:20.440 --> 02:01:24.839
Right? It is both true that that's,
that in the US, you know,

02:01:25.239 --> 02:01:28.339
Black populations have higher rates of hypertension. It
is also true that if you (laughs) go

02:01:28.459 --> 02:01:31.539
anywhere else that doesn't have a history
of racism, guess what?

02:01:31.539 --> 02:01:31.899
Yeah.

02:01:31.899 --> 02:01:35.600
They don't. And it's, um, same with
Native- Native Americans, uh,

02:01:35.719 --> 02:01:37.879
have higher rates of cardiovascular
disease in the US.

02:01:38.520 --> 02:01:42.679
Go to places where, um, uh, the hearty, the, the
healthiest hearts in the world

02:01:43.219 --> 02:01:47.019
are in, um, Indigenous populations
in Bolivia. Right?

02:01:47.339 --> 02:01:50.640
So, and it's basically a very similar,
you know, recent, uh, d-

02:01:51.100 --> 02:01:55.780
uh, diaspora of those populations down into South Af- South
America. So you're talking about genetically

02:01:55.879 --> 02:01:58.159
kind of similar populations, if you wanna
talk about it that way.

02:01:58.159 --> 02:01:58.979
Right.

02:01:58.979 --> 02:01:59.199
Um,

02:01:59.839 --> 02:02:00.039
so

02:02:01.120 --> 02:02:02.539
point being, um,

02:02:03.219 --> 02:02:08.199
we take the wrong conclusions, we make the wrong
fixes, we learn the wrong lessons

02:02:08.739 --> 02:02:13.059
when we don't understand how our bodies work
and how, why we become unique.

02:02:13.059 --> 02:02:14.579
And to your point of the,

02:02:15.179 --> 02:02:16.699
the German charity-

02:02:16.699 --> 02:02:16.899
Yeah.

02:02:16.899 --> 02:02:18.759
... this can be done well hearted.

02:02:18.759 --> 02:02:19.259
Yeah.

02:02:19.259 --> 02:02:20.479
Right? It's just not, uh,

02:02:21.899 --> 02:02:24.039
again, potentially not focusing
the right lens-

02:02:24.039 --> 02:02:24.359
That's right.

02:02:24.359 --> 02:02:25.819
... and help and support-

02:02:25.819 --> 02:02:25.999
Yeah.

02:02:25.999 --> 02:02:30.899
... in the areas, 'cause we don't understand what the actual
rationale, what the reason, what the cause-

02:02:30.899 --> 02:02:31.539
Yeah.

02:02:31.539 --> 02:02:33.919
... is for some of these issues. So, on

02:02:34.659 --> 02:02:36.179
the topic then of

02:02:37.659 --> 02:02:39.179
diversity and physiology-

02:02:39.179 --> 02:02:39.299
Yeah.

02:02:39.299 --> 02:02:41.519
... right, we'll, we'll stick again to
our side of the equation.

02:02:43.739 --> 02:02:47.319
How do we best think about that? What
is the best way we can

02:02:48.299 --> 02:02:49.279
learn from it, make

02:02:50.279 --> 02:02:51.419
better help-

02:02:51.419 --> 02:02:51.599
Mm-hmm.

02:02:51.599 --> 02:02:55.119
... better support, whether it's scientifically,
public health. I know you don't work in-

02:02:55.119 --> 02:02:55.259
Yeah.

02:02:55.259 --> 02:02:56.159
... areas of public health, but

02:02:56.699 --> 02:03:00.219
what is the most appropriate way that we should at
lea- maybe start thinking about it, or

02:03:00.979 --> 02:03:03.599
advocating for types of science?
Like, a- anything that's

02:03:04.179 --> 02:03:07.179
the solution side of the equation, how should
we be better thinking about it?

02:03:07.179 --> 02:03:10.559
Yeah. I think, uh, when we understand

02:03:10.999 --> 02:03:15.219
the role o- of environment especially in a lot of
these, uh, health outcome, like d-

02:03:15.619 --> 02:03:21.579
uh, diabetes risk, obesity risk, heart
disease risk, um, uh, I, I think

02:03:21.759 --> 02:03:23.639
a kinda individualized approach and a

02:03:23.719 --> 02:03:27.219
life course approach, because we're thinking about
environment over the lifespan here-

02:03:27.219 --> 02:03:27.319
Yep.

02:03:27.319 --> 02:03:31.759
... okay, um, points us to research
that's gonna focus on early

02:03:31.839 --> 02:03:36.119
life experiences especially, getting those
environments right, right? Childhood is a really

02:03:36.259 --> 02:03:40.499
important, obviously learning period for
your body's physiology. Um,

02:03:41.139 --> 02:03:42.699
and, you know, I think

02:03:44.019 --> 02:03:47.699
using, you know, w- when we see differences
among different communities in,

02:03:47.899 --> 02:03:51.519
in health outcomes, not ascribing that to
some inherent issue there-

02:03:51.519 --> 02:03:51.559
Mm-hmm.

02:03:51.559 --> 02:03:56.339
... but, uh, flagging that as, usually when it's at the
population level, an environmental issue.

02:03:56.739 --> 02:03:59.079
When we see individual differences,
well now, we can

02:03:59.199 --> 02:04:02.719
ask, "Okay, what is it about your individual physiology
that's, that's, that we need to tweak?"

02:04:03.579 --> 02:04:06.519
That's a move towards an, you know, kind of
precision medicine that I don't think,

02:04:07.299 --> 02:04:09.519
you know, uh, I don't have all the
answers there yet.

02:04:09.519 --> 02:04:09.599
Of course.

02:04:09.599 --> 02:04:10.699
I don't know that anybody does.

02:04:10.699 --> 02:04:10.999
No.

02:04:10.999 --> 02:04:14.019
But that's, we can focus more on the
individual that way and, and hopefully

02:04:14.619 --> 02:04:16.239
do, do a better job.

02:04:16.239 --> 02:04:17.479
It's, again, I'll never get over this

02:04:17.579 --> 02:04:22.439
interesting, what I feel like is a juxtaposition of what
your academic background (laughs) is-

02:04:22.439 --> 02:04:22.699
Yeah.

02:04:22.699 --> 02:04:24.919
... and the fact that you're now
arguing so heavily for,

02:04:25.819 --> 02:04:29.159
I'm sure it feels like it's seamless and is the
same thing, but I'm like, finally.

02:04:29.159 --> 02:04:29.479
(laughs)

02:04:29.479 --> 02:04:32.579
Yes, a person that's telling us all
about how we can learn

02:04:33.199 --> 02:04:34.839
from studying one tribe-

02:04:34.839 --> 02:04:34.959
Yeah.

02:04:34.959 --> 02:04:36.739
... 1,000 years ago is now getting it here.

